Z||000005||terryk||08-20-99||09:52 PM||||Actually, I have been looking at it in parallel with the turbo project. The problem is room. On top, on the sides. Limited. <p>I have a couple of Eaton M-62 roots style from my Ciera project. So far, it will take a custom adapter plate/fuel rail to sit on the top of the engine. The intake runs right into the distributor, so a custom pipe will be needed to bend around it. <p>Next is the belt drive. Not a lot of room over there. Either a longer belt or an additional drive pulley. Stacking another pulley on the water pump or crank is a tight fit.<p>Once we work out the adapter plate with integrated fuel rail, the rest should be easier.<p>As far a which one? If I could fit a Paxton or Vortec (prefered) on the side, I would. It might fit on the top rear, but it is a tight fit there.<p>I'm working with the Eaton since I have a couple.<p>The ECM will be the same as the turbo project, the 1227749 that drives the Syclone and Sunbird GT Turbo.  <p>It's not going to be a cakewalk and it the project could stop dead in it's tracks. Frankly, a turbo is all around easier.
Z||000006||Pretender||08-21-99||08:09 AM||||i've got an "high performance Pontiac" mag. with a silver 87 thats got a vortech/paxton unit in it, runs about 8 pounds of boost to make about 220-250 hp. He did all his fabricating at his shop in NewJersey. he ran the inlet piping on the firewall side back into the throttle body. i'll look for it and post the entire article...:0)
Z||000007||Shaun41178||08-22-99||02:09 AM||||Pretender:  I beleive it is the Feb 92 that has that article in it.  I would really like to have that article as I have been looking for it for a long time.  I know you said you were going to post the article to the list but I want to see pics of how it was done if they are in the mag.  I am more then willing to pay shipping if needed to have a xerox copy of that entire article.<p>By seeing pics of how it was done I am more then able to fab up my own supercharger system as the mounting brakcet will be the hardest part of the whole job for me as everything else is almost childs play.  Thanks<br>Shaun
