Z||000010||Pretender||08-22-99||10:23 PM||||I found it! <p>From the moment the fiero hit the streets in "84 performance enthusiasts have been beging for more power. For '85 the factory dropped the 2.8 liter v-6 into the engine bay, but it still left many P-car enthusiasts wanting more. When it became clear that help from PMD wasn't forthcoming, the Fiero faithful turned to the aftermarket.<p>There were loads of body kits, spoilers,fender flares and such on the market-so thatno 2 fieros need to look the same. But under the hood(lid?) it was a different story. Any performance parts available for the 2.8 would work on the fiero, but the pickings were slim. Then along came the turbocharger manufactures and things started to get interesting.<p>However, turbos bring certin problems such as intense heat, plumbing headaches and intake charge heating. These obstacles can all be overcome, but for the one man, there was a different solution to the lack of P-car power.<p>Randy O'Donahue of Haskell, N.J., decided that a well paxton supercharger was just the kind of attitude his '86 se needed. Randy was one of the first to experiment with mounting a supercharger in a fiero, since cramped quarters in the fiero engine compartment make installation difficult.<p>Randy overcame these installation problems with the help of his own machine shop. By machining the proper brackets himself, Randy assured a perfect, no hassle install. The blower is driven by a jack spindle that runs from the accessory drive belts at the front of the engine to the rear of the engine. This spindle is mounted on two high speed bearings, and has been trouble free after several houndred miles of break in driving.<p>The Blower is a stock paxton unit with an exturnal oiling system designed to increase longevty and is supplemented by a throttle controlled electronic water injection system. It delivers 8 pounds of boost to a basically stock engine. The only mods to the original V6 are 1.6:1 roller rockers, a custom built exhaust system, cold air intake using a K&N filter and an Accel-coiled MSD ignition with a rev. limiter.<p>Soon however randy will build a new engine for the car with an eye toward increased durability and power. A new O'Donahue designed super charger is also in the works: it will featurea redesigned bearing assembly and compressor weel. These two improvements over the currently available blowers will make it more durable piece and allow for increased boost pressure.<p>The cars performance already speaks for itself. While it had not been to the track as of this writing, during some street altercations with a modified late model mustang the silver bullet humbled the 5 oh in true pontiac fashion. Randy feels that twith the new blower the car will be capable of high 12's.<p>The rest of randys rocket is wrapped up a front spoiler, over the roof snorkles and ultra 16x7 wheels, while goodyear 225/60/vr16 tires put the car in touch with the pavement. Both the front and rear suspensions were dropped to improve handling and Bilstein shocks and an Addco rear sway bar help cope with the additional power. Inside the only deviations from stock are a boost gauge and a souped up stereo.<p>One ride in Randys car will convince any doubters taht the potential of the P-car was never realized in factory form. With a top speed of too damn fast, the O'Donahue Fiero is sure to stir more interest in Pontiac's plastic fantastic.<p>"IF you are interested in blowing your Fiero's mind just like randy did you can contact him at O'Donahue Racing, 14 Doty Rd., Haskell, N.J 07420: 1-201-835-2622"<p>i'll see what i can do with the pic's, or i could just send you the whole mag.,(no price of coarse)..email me about it...Pretender.
Z||000011||terryk||08-23-99||12:48 AM||||The Paxton is what, about $1500? It sounds like a $2500 effort. Not bad considering the improvement. That is the one I remember. <p>I also seem to remember the A/C needed to be removed. I could be wrong.<br>
Z||000012||Shaun41178||08-24-99||02:54 AM||||pretender: check your mail<p>Hey if the a/c compressor has to be removed to install the supercharger then so be it.  I will be losing aobut 75 pounds out of the rear and gain 80 hp at the same time.  A good trade off to me since I never use my a/c cause i have no need to.<p>Yes getting the compressor will be the most expensive part of the project as just the compressor can run upwards of $1000 new.    Also it is hard to find a used one and they don't sit in junkyards either.  Fabricating up brackets, adding retard control and intake piping and oil lines etc etc should run under $600 or so.  So for $1600 you can have one potent street Fiero.  Still a high price but cheaper then a kit which by the way is not offered for the Fiero V6.
Z||000013||Pretender||08-24-99||03:55 AM||||from the pic's of the engine bay it looks that the a/c can be kept., the spindle is mounted along the trunk side from an aditional pully probably added to the crank infront of the underdrive pully. But swaping out the a/c if you don't use it is another good idea to lose those extra added pounds abd gain a little bit more performance.
